ty affordable childcare it I'm  sure is not lost on the audience that we  are to women appear having this  conversation and I want to close by by  another confession I guess which is when  I was asked to do this interview I  looked at it because I knew I would want  to read your book and delighted to get  the chance to talk to you but there was  a tiny part of me thinking should give  this to a man which speaks to early  earlier point about having men in this  conversation how many of the book  interviews have you done that have been  with meal a couple I've done a couple we  read Zakaria has both Andy and me know  this weekend and Tom Ashbrook I've done  a couple and I'm getting lots of  responses from men interesting yesterday  I did a radio show where a lot of men  called in and talked about how they're  just as competent at home and they don't  like the assumption that they're not I I  do think that this conversation is ready  to be broken open by men and part of it  is men are missing out right one of the  things my husband said he wrote this  article in the Atlantic why I put my  wife's career first and he says it was  hard on his career it was better for his  marriage it was better for our sons and  it was better for him that he has a  relationship with them that his father  never had with him that he feel he has  grounded it's sort of the anchor of our  family in a way that I think women  that's part of what we get out of being  mothers and also being for  professionals so why shouldn't men have  the equal ability to really enjoy what  fatherhood's about or to be with your  own parents as they are at toward the  end of their lives it's not a burden  when it works it's the greatest thing in  the world we just need to make it work  for everybody an empowering message  Amory slaughter  thank you thank you so much on our road  to the White House coverage this evening  will take
 

